#doc (Manual) "Hole shroud" =>

# Hole Shroud

The shroud page belongs after causal order and the kinematic diamond-space
bridge. A hole is not admitted here as a primitive physical object. In this
phase it is a no-readout region generated by causal settling and a chosen
readout region.

The claim-site carried here is:

* claim-site: finite hole shroud and shroud-sourced effect object;
* phase: relational spacetime;
* generating sites: settled causal order, causal regions, future-closed regions,
  causal-diamond kinematic space, readout reachability, shroud adjacency, and
  shroud-supported effects;
* downstream uses: hole frontier diagnostics, dense-matter boundary
  comparisons, shroud/cohomology questions, hole-region causal diamonds, and
  keratinous-disc frontier pages;
* Lean carriers: `Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Hole`,
  `Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.HoleDiamondCausalSet`, and
  `Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon`;
* Blueprint/proof-tree nodes: `thm:hole-region`,
  `thm:hole-future-closed`, and `thm:hole-shroud`; richer
  descent-and-effects nodes remain frontier;
* provenance: the hole frontier asks for a finite no-readout region before
  any geometric or color theorem is made;
* familiar comparison: event horizon as a later physical reading of a no-readout
  shroud;
* reader closure: read "shroud" here as finite no-readout boundary, not as a
  smooth null surface; read "kinematic" through the sequencing causal-diamond
  page, not through a background continuum;
* frontier status: the causal `Hole` carrier is settled here with a
  shroud-supported effect relation. Descent obstruction, richer persistence,
  entropy, and any eventual coloring theorem remain frontier.

The governing sentence is:

> The finite hole region is the complement of causal readout.

## Causal Void

The name for the mathematically void structure is now explicit:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.CausalVoid}

It is built from the same data as the no-readout construction:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.CausalVoid.ofReadout}

The void laws are the closure and separation laws of the readout cut. In
particular, no void-interior event causally precedes a readout-exterior event:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.CausalVoid.no_future_to_exterior}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E) :
    CausalVoid E :=
  CausalVoid.ofReadout C readout

example {E : Type} (V : CausalVoid E) {x y : E}
    (hin : V.interior x = true)
    (hout : V.exterior y = true) :
    Not (CausalLe V.C x y) :=
  CausalVoid.no_future_to_exterior V hin hout
```

A `Hole` carries this void directly:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Hole.causalVoid}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Hole.causalVoid_no_future_to_exterior}

## Readout And No Readout

The readout predicate is:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.ReachesReadout}

The no-readout region is:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.HoleRegion}

and the exterior is its readout side:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.ReadoutExterior}

The definition is deliberately finite. It needs a causal set and a region
playing readout. It does not need a smooth manifold, metric tensor, conformal
boundary, or color theorem.

## Closure Laws

The main law is future closure:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.hole_future_closed}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E)
    {x y : E}
    (hx : HoleRegion C readout x = true)
    (hxy : CausalLe C x y) :
    HoleRegion C readout y = true :=
  hole_future_closed C readout hx hxy
```

The readout exterior is past-closed:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.readoutExterior_past_closed}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E)
    {x y : E}
    (hxy : CausalLe C x y)
    (hy : ReadoutExterior C readout y = true) :
    ReadoutExterior C readout x = true :=
  readoutExterior_past_closed C readout hxy hy
```

Interior events do not causally precede exterior events:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.hole_no_future_to_exterior}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E)
    {x y : E}
    (hin : HoleRegion C readout x = true)
    (hout : ReadoutExterior C readout y = true) :
    Not (CausalLe C x y) :=
  hole_no_future_to_exterior C readout hin hout
```

These are the lawful claims. No-readout propagates to the future; readout
reachability propagates to the past. That is the causal-order skeleton of the
shroud.

## Boundary

The finite adjacency predicate is:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.CausalAdjacent}

The shroud is:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Shroud}

A shroud point lies in the hole region and has an exterior neighbor:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.shroud_in_hole}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.shroud_has_exterior_neighbour}

The exterior neighbor is forced to precede the shroud event:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.shroud_has_exterior_predecessor}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E)
    {x : E}
    (h : Shroud C readout x = true) :
    HoleRegion C readout x = true :=
  shroud_in_hole C readout h

example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E)
    {x : E}
    (h : Shroud C readout x = true) :
    ReadoutExterior C readout x = false :=
  shroud_not_exterior C readout h
```

The familiar word "horizon" is now only a later physical reading of these
finite laws. The theorem does not say that the shroud is null, smooth, area-bearing,
thermodynamic, semiclassical, or black-colored. Those are later frontier
promotions.

## Diamond Kinematic Space

A causal diamond becomes an event of its own only after the endpoints are
already listed causal events:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.DiamondPoint}

The nesting relation is not the kinematic causal order. It self-loops at every
diamond point:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.nesting_self_edge}

and therefore cannot supply the strict-order closure required by a causal set:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.nesting_no_strictOrderClosure_of_point}

The causal order Lean proves is sequencing: one diamond precedes another when the
first upper endpoint causally precedes the second lower endpoint.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.HoleDiamondKinematicSpace}

The bundled theorem records both sides: nesting is excluded, sequencing is
transitively closed, and every readout on the diamond space inherits the
ordinary hole/exterior cut laws.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.holeDiamond_kinematic_space}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] [DecidableRel C.reaches] :
    CausalSet (DiamondPoint C) :=
  HoleDiamondKinematicSpace C

example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] [DecidableEq E]
    [DecidableRel C.reaches]
    (readout : FiniteHoleRegion (DiamondPoint C)) :
    DiamondPoint C ->
      Discrete.Graph.StrictOrderClosure
        (nestingPrec0 C) ->
        False :=
  (holeDiamond_kinematic_space C readout).1
```

This is the kinematic space used by the hole/diamond Husainov frontier: it does
not prove Dedekind--MacNeille cut acyclicity, and it does not identify a
continuum moduli space. It proves the finite causal substrate on which those
questions can be asked.

## Deshrouding

A diamond point needs a genuine upper endpoint the lower endpoint strictly
reaches. Before that endpoint is settled, a currently witnessed shroud event
can stand in for it: it is inside the hole region, adjacent to the readout
exterior, but proven not itself exterior.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.deshroudedDiamondPoint}

The produced diamond's upper endpoint honestly does not overclaim completion:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.deshroudedDiamondPoint_upper_not_exterior}

And deshrouding a later-witnessed shroud event nests the earlier deshrouded
diamond under the same lower endpoint -- the proxy only grows as more of the
run is witnessed, it never shrinks or moves its admission:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.deshroudedDiamondPoint_nested_of_causalLe}

This names, without yet answering, what happens once the true readout is
finally reached: whether anything proved about the deshrouded proxy survives
the swap to the true diamond stays open here.

## Post-Hole Tiles

A computed hole region is not just a region -- it carries its own causal adjacency graph, and a
post-hole tile records a FOUR-valued colouring of that graph together with the future-core data
needed to certify the hole is a single connected piece.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.HoleTileAdjacency}

Two guards keep this adjacency graph honestly colourable. Causal adjacency is reflexive -- every
point is adjacent to itself -- so without excluding the diagonal, properness would demand an event
differ in colour from itself. And restricting to listed events matters once the causal set is
unbounded: comparability alone, without a listedness restriction, can range over chains longer than
a four-colour palette can cover.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.HoleTileColoring}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.PostHoleTile}

Checked, over a genuinely Mark-generated three-event chain -- the prefix chain of one real
`markStepCausalSet` top, packaged as a finite carrier so the connectivity machinery above applies to
what it was built for:

```lean
example : PostHoleTile dlCausalSet dlReadout :=
  dlPostHoleTile
```

The witness's future core is anchored where the colouring settles:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.dlAnchorCore}

## Connectedness, One Point At A Time

The Alexandrov-connectedness toolkit had exactly one generator of concrete connected sets: the
principal up-set. A one-point set is connected too, and for a different reason -- it has no
nontrivial separation to admit at all, not because it happens to be an up-set.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.Alexandrov.isConnected_singleton}

This closes the sibling obligation to the hole region's own connectedness: the readout exterior.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.ReadoutExteriorConnected}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.dlReadoutExteriorConnected}

Checked:

```lean
example : ReadoutExteriorConnected dlCausalSet dlReadout :=
  dlReadoutExteriorConnected
```

## The Diamond Lift

Does a post-hole tile lift from a causal set to its own space of causal diamonds? Two facts hold
completely generally, for any causal set at all.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.upperPoint_monotone}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.holeRegion_lift_pullback}

Checked, general over any causal set with the right decidability data:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] [DecidableEq E]
    [DecidableRel C.reaches]
    (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E) {p : DiamondPoint C}
    (hp :
      HoleRegion C readout p.upper.point = true) :
    HoleRegion (sequencingCausalSet C)
        (fun q => readout q.upper.point) p =
      true :=
  holeRegion_lift_pullback C readout hp
```

On the three-event witness chain the lifted colouring is proper:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.dlColoringD}

But the future core does not close, and not for lack of trying:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.dlDPJump_dlDPStep'_unrelated}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.dlDPJump_dlDPStep'_upperPoint_collapse}

The upper-endpoint map is not injective -- two distinct diamonds collapse onto the same base
event and hence the same colour -- and the two diamonds that collapse together are, at the same
time, causally unrelated to one another in the sequencing order. A colour coincidence between
causally unrelated events is invisible to properness; a connectivity failure between them is not.

Checked:

```lean
example :
    dlDPJump ≠ dlDPStep' ∧
      dlDPJump.upper.point =
        dlDPStep'.upper.point :=
  ⟨dlDPJump_ne_dlDPStep',
    dlDPJump_dlDPStep'_upperPoint_collapse⟩
```

## Flipping The Endpoint

Nothing in "an endpoint-reflecting lift" names a preferred endpoint. Reading a diamond's readout
membership and colour off its lower endpoint instead is the exact dual construction:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.lowerPoint_monotone}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.holeRegion_lift_pullback_lower}

On the same witness chain this single change of perspective closes the lift completely. The
lower-pullback hole collapses to one point:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.dlHoleRegionDLower_iff}

and a one-point hole is a post-hole tile for free -- no adjacency pairs to colour, and connected
by `isConnected_singleton` with no further argument needed:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.dlPostHoleTileDLower}

Checked:

```lean
example :
    PostHoleTile
      (sequencingCausalSet dlCausalSet)
      dlReadoutDLower :=
  dlPostHoleTileDLower
```

So the diamond-lift closure question does not have a single yes-or-no answer at the level of a
fixed endpoint choice. The witness chain's tile fails to lift through its upper endpoint and
succeeds through its lower one. What decides which endpoint closes -- and whether one of the two
always does, for every causal set and every tile -- is the open content behind the conjecture; this
page settles only the existence of one closing witness and one honestly-proved obstruction to the
other.

## Kugelblitz Frontier

The next articulation layer supplies a sharper frontier word: a kugelblitz step
is not just any hole step, and not just any diamond point. It is an
articulation-generated diamond whose lower endpoint is already in the readout
exterior and whose new upper endpoint lands on the shroud.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.DiamondCrossesReadoutHorizon}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.ArticulationKugelblitzStep}

The object is an articulation site: a tail plus the next utterance that grows
the chain by one paired articulation.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite.next}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite.diamondPoint}

This direction matters. The existing hole laws forbid an interior event from
causally preceding an exterior event, so the finite horizon-crossing direction
available here is exterior to shroud-side interior. The definition immediately
yields the endpoint readouts:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite.tail_exterior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite.next_shroud}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite.next_in_hole}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite.tail_not_in_hole}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite.next_not_exterior}

The unbundled theorem names are also exposed for direct step use:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.articulationKugelblitz_tail_exterior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.articulationKugelblitz_pair_shroud}

and the shroud endpoint is in the computed hole region:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.articulationKugelblitz_pair_in_hole}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.articulationKugelblitz_tail_not_in_hole}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.articulationKugelblitz_pair_not_exterior}

The proved local entry law is a switch in the generated causal set: tail is
exterior and not hole-side; the next articulation is shroud-side, hole-side,
and not exterior.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.ArticulationHoleRegionSwitchesAt}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.articulationKugelblitz_holeRegionSwitch}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.KugelblitzArticulationSite.holeRegionSwitch}

Checked:

```lean
example {readout : FiniteHoleRegion Articulating}
    (K : KugelblitzArticulationSite readout) :
    HoleRegion K.causalSet readout K.tail = false :=
  KugelblitzArticulationSite.tail_not_in_hole K

example {readout : FiniteHoleRegion Articulating}
    (K : KugelblitzArticulationSite readout) :
    ReadoutExterior K.causalSet readout K.next =
      false :=
  KugelblitzArticulationSite.next_not_exterior K

example {readout : FiniteHoleRegion Articulating}
    (K : KugelblitzArticulationSite readout) :
    ArticulationHoleRegionSwitchesAt
      readout K.utterance K.tail :=
  KugelblitzArticulationSite.holeRegionSwitch K
```

The growth side is stated separately:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.ArticulationHoleRegionStrictlyGrowsAt}

The conjecture is the exactness claim:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.kugelblitz_growth_conjecture}

This is not yet a theorem. It records the frontier shaped by the notes: causal
diamonds can be treated as region-points, but dynamics or growth laws on that
space are not imported from the literature. Here the proposed dynamics is the
local articulation law: strict hole-region growth happens exactly when the next
articulation diamond crosses the readout horizon.

## Event Horizon

The event-horizon carrier packages the finite causal substrate, readout, named
surface, and proof that the surface is exactly the shroud:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.EventHorizon}

The canonical constructor builds that carrier from a causal set and readout:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.EventHorizon.ofReadout}

Its surface laws expose the horizon as a causal boundary:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.EventHorizon.surface_interior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.EventHorizon.surface_not_exterior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.EventHorizon.interior_no_future_to_exterior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.EventHorizon.surface_has_exterior_predecessor}

and proof-carrying events can be tested against that surface:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.EventHorizon.event_surface_interior}

## Horizon Frontier Map

The external names are now routed through a small finite map. The black-hole
region proxy is not new geometry; it is the existing no-readout region:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.BlackHoleRegion}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.blackHole_future_closed}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E)
    {x y : E}
    (hx : BlackHoleRegion C readout x = true)
    (hxy : CausalLe C x y) :
    BlackHoleRegion C readout y = true :=
  blackHole_future_closed C readout hx hxy
```

An apparent event is local and finite: a shroud event, not a continuum trapped
surface or apparent horizon.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.ApparentEvent}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.ApparentEvent.in_blackHoleRegion}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.ApparentEvent.not_exterior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.ApparentEvent.has_exterior_predecessor}

Readout relativity is carried as a generated horizon package:

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.ReadoutRelativeHorizon}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.readoutRelativeHorizon_surface_interior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.readoutRelativeHorizon_surface_eq_shroud}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.readoutRelativeHorizon_interior_eq_blackHoleRegion}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.readoutRelativeHorizon_exterior_eq_readoutExterior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.readoutRelativeHorizon_surface_not_exterior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.readoutRelativeHorizon_surface_has_exterior_predecessor}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E)
    {x : E}
    (hx :
      (ReadoutRelativeHorizon C readout).surface x = true) :
    (ReadoutRelativeHorizon C readout).interior x =
      true :=
  readoutRelativeHorizon_surface_interior C readout hx

example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C] (readout : FiniteHoleRegion E)
    {x : E}
    (hx :
      (ReadoutRelativeHorizon C readout).surface x = true) :
    (ReadoutRelativeHorizon C readout).exterior x =
      false :=
  readoutRelativeHorizon_surface_not_exterior C readout hx
```

The finite SO readout frame keeps that readout-generated horizon and adds only
a finite orientation-frame proxy: a finite gauge group, connection, local
transform, class-function observable, and closed loop. This is the finite
frame-gauge law available at the horizon, not a smooth SO bundle.

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.FiniteSOReadoutFrame}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.FiniteSOReadoutFrame.horizon_surface_eq_shroud}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.FiniteSOReadoutFrame.horizon_interior_eq_blackHoleRegion}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.FiniteSOReadoutFrame.horizon_exterior_eq_readoutExterior}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.FiniteSOReadoutFrame.closedLoop_holonomy_conjugates}

{docstring Obsmetrologia.CausalSets.BlackHoles.Horizon.FiniteSOReadoutFrame.wilsonLoop_invariant}

Checked:

```lean
example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C]
    {readout : FiniteHoleRegion E}
    (F : FiniteSOReadoutFrame readout) (x : E) :
    (ReadoutRelativeHorizon C readout).surface x =
      Shroud C readout x :=
  FiniteSOReadoutFrame.horizon_surface_eq_shroud
    C F x

example {E : Type} (C : CausalSet E)
    [CausalLeDecision C]
    {readout : FiniteHoleRegion E}
    (F : FiniteSOReadoutFrame readout) (x : E) :
    (ReadoutRelativeHorizon C readout).interior x =
      BlackHoleRegion C readout x :=
  FiniteSOReadoutFrame.horizon_interior_eq_blackHoleRegion
    C F x

example {E : Type}
    {readout : FiniteHoleRegion E}
    (F : FiniteSOReadoutFrame readout) :
    F.observable.f
        (DenseMatter.Quark.holo F.orientationGroup
          (DenseMatter.Quark.transformEdge
            F.orientationGroup
            F.connection
            F.transform)
          F.base F.loop) =
      F.observable.f
        (DenseMatter.Quark.holo F.orientationGroup
          F.connection F.base F.loop) :=
  FiniteSOReadoutFrame.wilsonLoop_invariant F
```

The antihole side is the order-dual no-ingress carrier: the complement of the
readout future, not a continuum time-reversed spacetime.

